Article 22 For the following cases, a People's Procuratorate at a higher level may designate jurisdiction:
(1) Cases over which jurisdiction is disputed;
(2) Cases for which jurisdiction needs to be changed;
(3) Specific types of cases for which centralized jurisdiction is needed;
(4) Other cases for which jurisdiction needs to be designated.
Where the review and prosecution of a case referred to in the preceding paragraph is designated for jurisdiction, the People's Procuratorate shall reach an agreement with the corresponding People's Court. Where the review and arrest of a case referred to in item (3) of the preceding paragraph is designated for jurisdiction, the People's Procuratorate shall reach an agreement with the corresponding public security organ.

Article 48 From the date on which the People's Procuratorate reviews the case for prosecution, if a defender other than a lawyer applies to the People's Procuratorate to consult, extract, or duplicate the case file materials, or to meet and correspond with the criminal suspect in custody or under residential surveillance, the department responsible for arrest and prosecution of the People's Procuratorate shall review the application and decide whether to grant permission, and shall notify the applicant in writing within three days.
If the People's Procuratorate permits a defender other than a lawyer to correspond with a criminal suspect in custody or under residential surveillance, it may request the detention house or the public security organ to submit the correspondence to the People's Procuratorate for inspection.
Where a defender other than a lawyer applies to consult, extract, or duplicate case file materials, or to meet and correspond with a criminal suspect in custody or under residential surveillance, the People's Procuratorate may refuse permission if any of the following circumstances exists:
(1) the co-suspect in the same case is at large;
(2) the facts of the case are unclear, the evidence is insufficient, or there are omitted crimes or omitted co-suspects that require supplementary investigation;
(3) the case involves state secrets or trade secrets;
(4) there are facts indicating the possibility of collusion, destruction or fabrication of evidence, or endangering the personal safety of a witness.

Article 57 Where a defender or litigation agent believes that a public security organ, the People's Procuratorate, the People's Court, or their staff members have committed any of the following acts that obstruct the defender or litigation agent from lawfully exercising their litigation rights, and files a petition or complaint with the People's Procuratorate at the same level or the next higher level, the department responsible for petition and prosecution of the People's Procuratorate shall accept and handle it according to law, and other case-handling departments shall cooperate:
(1) failing, in violation of provisions, to accept a request for withdrawal filed by a defender or litigation agent, or failing to accept a review application regarding a decision not to withdraw;
(2) failing to inform, according to law, the criminal suspect or defendant of the right to retain a defender;
(3) failing to convey the request of a criminal suspect or defendant in custody or under residential surveillance for retaining a defender, or failing to forward his or her application for legal aid materials;
(4) failing, when obligated, to notify a legal aid institution to assign a lawyer to provide defense or legal aid for a criminal suspect or defendant who meets the conditions, or for a person who is subject to an application for compulsory medical treatment;
(5) failing, within the prescribed time, to accept or respond to a defender's application for modification of compulsory measures or request for removal of compulsory measures;
(6) failing to inform the defense lawyer, according to law, of the crime suspected of the criminal suspect and the relevant information of the case;
(7) illegally restricting the defense lawyer from meeting and corresponding with the criminal suspect or defendant in custody or under residential surveillance;
(8) illegally refusing to allow the defense lawyer to consult, extract, or duplicate the case file materials of the case;
(9) illegally restricting the defense lawyer from collecting or verifying relevant evidence materials;
(10) without justifiable reasons, refusing to agree to the defense lawyer's application to collect or obtain evidence or to notify a witness to testify in court, or failing to reply or explain the reasons;
(11) failing to submit, according to law, evidence materials proving that the criminal suspect or defendant is innocent or has a lesser crime;
(12) failing to hear, according to law, the opinions of the defender or litigation agent;
(13) failing to notify the defender or litigation agent of the time and place of the court session in a timely manner, according to law;
(14) failing to serve promptly, according to law, the legal documents of the case to the defender or litigation agent, or failing to inform them promptly of the transfer of the case;
(15) obstructing the defender or litigation agent from exercising their litigation rights according to law during the court trial;
(16) other acts obstructing the defender or litigation agent from lawfully exercising their litigation rights.
Where a petition or complaint is filed directly with the People's Procuratorate at the next higher level, the People's Procuratorate at the next higher level may refer it to the lower-level People's Procuratorate for handling, or may handle it directly.
Where a defender or litigation agent believes that a detention house or its staff members have committed acts obstructing them from lawfully exercising their litigation rights and files a petition or complaint with the People's Procuratorate, the department responsible for criminal execution inspection shall accept and handle it according to law; if other case-handling departments receive the petition or complaint, they shall promptly transfer it to the department responsible for criminal execution inspection.

Article 63 For cases where the People's Procuratorate concludes an investigation or institutes a public prosecution, the evidence shall be truthful and sufficient. Evidence being truthful and sufficient shall satisfy the following conditions:
(1) All facts concerning conviction and sentencing are supported by evidence;
(2) All evidence on which the case is determined has been verified as true through legally prescribed procedures;
(3) Comprehensive evaluation of all evidence in the case has eliminated any reasonable doubt regarding the facts determined.

Article 67 Confessions of a criminal suspect obtained through the following methods shall be excluded:
(1) Confessions made against the suspect's will by causing unbearable pain through violent methods such as beating, unlawful use of restraint devices, or other means of disguised corporal punishment;
(2) Confessions made against the suspect's will by causing unbearable pain through threats of violence or serious harm to the lawful rights and interests of the suspect or their close relatives;
(3) Confessions obtained through illegal methods of restricting personal freedom, such as unlawful detention.

Article 68 Where a criminal suspect is made to give a confession by means of extorting confessions by torture, subsequent repeated confessions that are identical to that confession and are made under the influence of such torture shall also be excluded, except in the following circumstances:
(1) During the investigation period, where, based on complaints, reports, or self-discovery, the public security organ confirms that evidence was obtained by illegal means or cannot exclude such possibility, and the investigators are replaced; when other investigators conduct another interrogation, they inform the suspect of his litigation rights and the legal provisions on admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment, and the criminal suspect voluntarily makes a confession;
(2) During the period of review of arrest or review of prosecution, where the prosecutor, during interrogation, informs the criminal suspect of his litigation rights and the legal provisions on admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment, and the criminal suspect voluntarily makes a confession.

Article 75 For cases filed for investigation by the public security organ, where any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Procuratorate may, during the review of arrest, review of prosecution, or trial stage, obtain audio and video recordings of the public security organ's interrogation of the criminal suspect to review the legality of the evidence collection and the truthfulness of the confessions of the criminal suspect or defendant:
(1) It is believed that the interrogation may have involved illegal evidence collection such as extorting confessions by torture;
(2) The criminal suspect, defendant, or defender claims that the confession of the criminal suspect or defendant was obtained illegally and provides relevant clues or materials;
(3) The criminal suspect or defendant claims that the interrogation violated statutory procedures or retracts the confession, and provides relevant clues or materials;
(4) The criminal suspect, defendant, or defender claims that the content of the interrogation record is untrue and provides relevant clues or materials;
(5) The case is major, difficult, or complex.
Where the People's Procuratorate obtains audio and video recordings of the public security organ's interrogation of the criminal suspect, but the public security organ does not provide them, and after review, the People's Procuratorate finds that it cannot exclude the possibility of illegal evidence collection such as extorting confessions by torture, the relevant confessions shall not be used as the basis for approving arrest or instituting a public prosecution.
For cases directly accepted for investigation by the People's Procuratorate, when the department responsible for investigation refers the case for review of arrest or for prosecution, it shall transfer the audio and video recordings of the interrogation together with the case file materials for review.

Article 79 When handling cases involving crimes of endangering national security, terrorist activities, organized crimes of a mafia nature, drug crimes, and the like, if a witness, expert evaluator, or victim requests protection from the People's Procuratorate because their personal safety or that of their close relatives is in danger due to testifying in the proceedings, the People's Procuratorate shall accept the request and promptly conduct a review. If there is indeed a danger to personal safety, it shall immediately take necessary protective measures. If the People's Procuratorate discovers the existence of the aforementioned circumstances, it shall proactively take protective measures.
The People's Procuratorate may take one or more of the following protective measures:
(1) Not disclosing personal information such as the true name, address, and work unit;
(2) Suggesting that the court take measures when testifying in court to conceal appearance and true voice;
(3) Prohibiting specific persons from contacting the witness, expert evaluator, victim, and their close relatives;
(4) Taking specialized protective measures for personal safety and residence;
(5) Other necessary protective measures.
Where the People's Procuratorate decides according to law not to disclose personal information such as the true name, address, and work unit of the witness, expert evaluator, or victim, pseudonyms may be used in legal documents and evidence materials such as the indictment and interrogation records. However, a separate written explanation of the use of pseudonyms shall be provided, marked with the classification level, and kept in a separate file.
When taking protective measures according to law, the People's Procuratorate may request the relevant entities and individuals to cooperate.
If threats, insults, beatings, or retaliation against the witness or their close relatives constitute a crime or warrant public security punishment, the People's Procuratorate shall transfer the case to the public security organ for handling; if the circumstances are minor, criticism, education, or a reprimand shall be given.

Article 86 The People's Procuratorate may grant release on bail pending investigation or trial to a criminal suspect under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The criminal suspect may be sentenced to public surveillance, criminal detention, or a supplementary punishment independently applicable;
(2) The criminal suspect may be sentenced to fixed-term imprisonment or a more severe punishment, and granting release on bail pending investigation or trial would not create any danger to society;
(3) The criminal suspect suffers from a serious illness, cannot take care of himself or herself, or is a pregnant woman or a woman nursing her own infant, and granting release on bail pending investigation or trial would not create any danger to society;
(4) The period of custody has expired, the case has not yet been concluded, and release on bail pending investigation or trial is necessary.

Article 89 When the People's Procuratorate decides to grant release on bail pending investigation or trial to a criminal suspect, the criminal suspect shall be ordered to provide a guarantor or pay a deposit.
For the same criminal suspect, the methods of guarantor guarantee and deposit guarantee shall not be used simultaneously.
If a criminal suspect meets the conditions for release on bail pending investigation or trial and falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Procuratorate may, when deciding to grant release on bail pending investigation or trial, order him or her to provide one to two guarantors:
(1) Unable to pay a deposit;
(2) A minor or a person who has reached the age of seventy-five;
(3) Other circumstances where it is inappropriate to collect a deposit.

Article 93 When the People's Procuratorate decides to grant release on bail pending investigation or trial to a criminal suspect, it shall produce a written decision on release on bail pending investigation or trial, specifying the start time of the release on bail pending investigation or trial, the method of guarantee, and the obligations to be performed and the provisions to be complied with by the person released on bail pending investigation or trial.
When making the decision on release on bail pending investigation or trial, the People's Procuratorate may, in light of the nature of the crime suspected, the harmful consequences, the social impact, and the specific circumstances of the criminal suspect, the victim, etc., specifically order the criminal suspect to comply with one or more of the following provisions:
(1) Not to enter specific places;
(2) Not to meet or correspond with specific persons;
(3) Not to engage in specific activities;
(4) To deliver passports, other entry-exit documents, and driving licenses to the executing authority for safekeeping.

Article 101 Where a criminal suspect commits any of the following acts in violation of the provisions on release on bail pending investigation or trial, the People's Procuratorate shall arrest the criminal suspect:
(1) intentionally committing a new crime;
(2) attempting suicide or escape;
(3) committing acts of destroying or falsifying evidence, colluding with others or interfering with witness testimony, sufficient to affect the normal progress of investigation and examination of prosecution;
(4) retaliating against victims, witnesses, expert evaluators, informants, accusers, or other persons.
Where a criminal suspect commits any of the following acts in violation of the provisions on release on bail pending investigation or trial, the People's Procuratorate may arrest the criminal suspect:
(1) leaving the city or county where he or she resides without approval, causing serious consequences, or twice leaving without approval;
(2) failing to appear before the authorities when summoned, causing serious consequences, or twice failing to appear when summoned;
(3) failing to report changes in address, work unit, or contact information to the public security organ within twenty-four hours, causing serious consequences;
(4) entering specific places, meeting or communicating with specific persons, or engaging in specific activities in violation of regulations, seriously obstructing the normal conduct of the proceedings.
In the circumstances set out in the preceding two paragraphs, if the criminal suspect needs to be arrested, he or she may be detained in advance; if a deposit has been paid, the public security organ shall be notified in writing to confiscate the deposit.

Article 107 The People's Procuratorate may place a criminal suspect under residential surveillance if the conditions for arrest are met and the criminal suspect falls under any of the following circumstances:
(1) suffering from a serious illness and unable to take care of himself or herself;
(2) being a pregnant woman or a woman nursing her own infant;
(3) being the sole person supporting a person who cannot take care of himself or herself;
(4) due to the special circumstances of the case or the needs of handling the case, residential surveillance is more appropriate;
(5) the period of custody expires, the case has not been concluded, and residential surveillance measures are necessary.
The term "support" in subparagraph (3) of the preceding paragraph includes the upbringing of children, grandchildren, and great-grandchildren by parents, paternal grandparents, maternal grandparents, and the maintenance of parents, paternal grandparents, maternal grandparents by children, grandchildren, and great-grandchildren, as well as mutual support between spouses and between siblings.
Where the conditions for release on bail pending investigation or trial are met, but the criminal suspect cannot provide a surety or pay a deposit, residential surveillance may be imposed.

Article 111 Where a criminal suspect commits any of the following acts in violation of the provisions on residential surveillance, the People's Procuratorate shall arrest the criminal suspect:
(1) intentionally committing a new criminal act;
(2) attempting suicide or escape;
(3) committing acts of destroying or falsifying evidence, colluding with others, or interfering with witness testimony, sufficient to affect the normal progress of investigation and examination of prosecution;
(4) retaliating against victims, witnesses, expert evaluators, informants, accusers, or other persons.
Where a criminal suspect commits any of the following acts in violation of the provisions on residential surveillance, the People's Procuratorate may arrest the criminal suspect:
(1) leaving the place of residential surveillance without approval, causing serious consequences, or twice leaving without approval;
(2) meeting others or communicating without approval, causing serious consequences, or twice meeting or communicating without approval;
(3) failing to appear before the authorities when summoned, causing serious consequences, or twice failing to appear when summoned.
In the circumstances set out in the preceding two paragraphs, if the criminal suspect needs to be arrested, he or she may be detained in advance.

Article 117 Where residential surveillance is executed at a designated residence, except where it is impossible to notify, the People's Procuratorate shall, within twenty-four hours after executing the residential surveillance, notify the family members of the person under residential surveillance of the reasons for the residential surveillance at a designated residence. If notification is impossible, the reasons shall be recorded in writing and attached to the case file. Once the impossibility of notification is eliminated, notification shall be given immediately.
"Impossibility of notification" includes the following circumstances:
(1) the person under residential surveillance has no family members;
(2) it is impossible to contact his or her family members;
(3) being hindered by force majeure such as natural disasters.

Article 119 If a person under residential surveillance at a designated residence, or his or her legal representative, near relative, or defender, considers that the decision on residential surveillance at a designated residence is illegal and files a complaint or report, the People's Procuratorate shall accept it.
The People's Procuratorate may request the relevant authority to provide the decision on residential surveillance at a designated residence and relevant case files. Upon review, if any of the following illegal circumstances are found, the authority shall be promptly notified to rectify:
(1) not meeting the applicable conditions for residential surveillance at a designated residence;
(2) failing to go through the approval procedures according to legal procedures;
(3) other acts violating the provisions of the Criminal Procedure Law during the decision-making process.

Article 120 For cases where the public security organ or the People's Court decides on residential surveillance at a designated residence, the department responsible for criminal execution inspection of the People's Procuratorate shall supervise the legality of the execution activities of residential surveillance at a designated residence. If any of the following illegal circumstances are found, corrective opinions shall be promptly issued:
(1) after receiving legal documents such as the decision on residential surveillance at a designated residence and the enforcement notice, the executing authority fails to dispatch personnel or does so untimely;
(2) failing to notify the family members of the person under residential surveillance within twenty-four hours after executing residential surveillance at a designated residence;
(3) executing residential surveillance in custodial venues or special case-handling venues;
(4) tipping off the person under residential surveillance, privately transmitting letters or articles;
(5) arranging meetings or communications between the defender and the person under residential surveillance in violation of regulations, or illegally restricting meetings or communications between the person under residential surveillance and the defender;
(6) extorting confessions by torture, corporal punishment, abuse, or disguised corporal punishment or abuse against the person under residential surveillance;
(7) other acts infringing on the lawful rights of the person under residential surveillance or other illegal acts.
If the person under residential surveillance, or his or her legal representative, near relative, or defender, considers that the executing authority or personnel have committed the above illegal acts and files a complaint or report, the People's Procuratorate shall accept it.
For cases where the People's Procuratorate decides on residential surveillance at a designated residence, the department responsible for prosecution of complaints and appeals shall supervise the legality of the execution activities of residential surveillance at a designated residence.

Article 121 A People's Procuratorate may decide to detain a criminal suspect under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The suspect attempts suicide, escapes or is at large after committing a crime;
(2) The suspect may destroy or falsify evidence or collude with others to make false statements.

Article 128 Where a People's Procuratorate considers that for a criminal suspect against whom there is evidence to substantiate the facts of a crime and who may be sentenced to a punishment of not less than fixed-term imprisonment, release on bail pending investigation is insufficient to prevent the following social dangers, it shall approve or decide to arrest:
(1) The suspect may commit a new crime;
(2) There is a real danger of endangering national security, public security, or social order;
(3) The suspect may destroy or falsify evidence, interfere with witness testimony, or collude with others to make false statements;
(4) The suspect may retaliate against the victim, informant, or accuser;
(5) The suspect attempts suicide or escape.
"Evidence to substantiate the facts of a crime" means the following circumstances are all present:
(1) There is evidence to prove that a criminal fact has occurred;
(2) There is evidence to prove that the criminal fact was committed by the criminal suspect;
(3) The evidence proving that the criminal suspect committed the criminal act has been verified.
The facts of a crime may be the facts of a single criminal act or the facts of any one of several criminal acts.

Article 129 Where a criminal suspect falls under any of the following circumstances, he may be found to be "likely to commit a new crime":
(1) Before or after the incident, the suspect is planning, organizing, or preparing to commit a new crime;
(2) The suspect openly declares that he will commit a new crime;
(3) The suspect has committed multiple offenses, consecutive offenses, or transitory offenses;
(4) Within one year, the suspect has been subjected to an administrative penalty for intentionally committing similar illegal acts;
(5) The suspect relies on criminal proceeds as his main source of living;
(6) The suspect has bad habits such as drug abuse or gambling;
(7) Other circumstances where a new crime may be committed.

Article 130 Where a criminal suspect falls under any of the following circumstances, he may be found to have "a real danger of endangering national security, public security, or social order":
(1) Before or after the incident, the suspect is actively planning, organizing, or preparing to commit a major illegal or criminal act endangering national security, public security, or social order;
(2) The suspect has previously received criminal punishment or administrative penalty for endangering national security, public security, or social order;
(3) The suspect plays an organizing, planning, commanding role or actively participates in crimes endangering national security, organized crime of a triad nature, terrorist activities, or drug-related crimes;
(4) Other circumstances where there is a real danger of endangering national security, public security, or social order.

Article 131 Where a criminal suspect falls under any of the following circumstances, he may be found to be "likely to destroy or falsify evidence, interfere with witness testimony, or collude with others to make false statements":
(1) The suspect has previously attempted to or attempted to destroy, falsify, conceal, or transfer evidence;
(2) The suspect has previously attempted to or attempted to intimidate, threaten, bribe, or buy off witnesses to interfere with their testimony;
(3) There are co-suspects or suspects who are in fact closely related to the crime who are at large, and important evidence has not yet been collected;
(4) Other circumstances where the suspect may destroy or falsify evidence, interfere with witness testimony, or collude with others to make false statements.

Article 132 Where a criminal suspect falls under any of the following circumstances, he may be found to be "likely to retaliate against the victim, informant, or accuser":
(1) The suspect openly declares, prepares, or plans to retaliate against the victim, informant, or accuser;
(2) The suspect has previously committed acts of retaliation, coercion, or persecution against the victim, informant, or accuser;
(3) The suspect engages in other forms of harassment against the normal life or work of the victim, informant, or accuser;
(4) Other circumstances where the suspect may retaliate against the victim, informant, or accuser.

Article 133 Where a criminal suspect falls under any of the following circumstances, he may be found to "attempt suicide or escape":
(1) The suspect has started preparing for suicide, self-harm, or escape;
(2) The suspect has previously committed suicide, self-harm, or escape;
(3) The suspect has expressed intention to commit suicide, self-harm, or escape;
(4) The suspect has previously resisted arrest by violent or threatening means;
(5) Other circumstances where the suspect attempts suicide or escape.

Article 138 For a criminal suspect who has committed multiple criminal acts or is involved in a joint crime, who meets the conditions of Article 128 of these Rules and falls under any of the following circumstances, the arrest shall be approved or decided:
(1) There is evidence to prove that the suspect has committed one of several crimes;
(2) There is evidence to prove that the suspect has committed one of multiple offenses;
(3) In a joint crime, there is already evidence to prove that the suspect committed the criminal fact.

Article 139 Where a criminal suspect falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Procuratorate shall decide not to approve the arrest or not to arrest:
(1) The conditions for arrest set forth in these Rules are not met;
(2) The circumstances specified in Article 16 of the Criminal Procedure Law apply.

Article 140 Where the crime suspected of being committed by a criminal suspect is relatively minor and there is no other major criminal suspicion, and the suspect falls under any of the following circumstances, a decision may be made not to approve the arrest or not to arrest:
(1) The suspect is a preparator or a desister, or acts in excessive defense or excessive necessity;
(2) The suspect is a first-time offender with relatively minor subjective malignancy, an accessory or coerced accomplice in a joint crime, and after the crime, voluntarily surrenders, performs meritorious acts, actively returns illegal gains, compensates for losses, or genuinely shows repentance;
(3) The suspect is a negligent offender who shows repentance after the crime, effectively controls losses, or actively compensates for losses;
(4) The suspect and the victim have reached a settlement agreement in accordance with the relevant provisions of the Criminal Procedure Law, and upon review, it is deemed that the settlement is voluntary, lawful, and has been performed or a guarantee has been provided;
(5) The suspect admits guilt and accepts punishment;
(6) The suspect is a minor who has reached the age of fourteen but under the age of eighteen, or is a student at school, and personally shows repentance, and his family, school, or the community, residents' committee, or villagers' committee have the conditions for guardianship and assistance;
(7) The suspect has reached the age of seventy-five.

Article 156 The following cases shall be uniformly accepted by the case management department of a People's Procuratorate:
(1) Cases submitted by public security organs for approval of arrest, referral for prosecution, approval of extension of the period of criminal custody during investigation, request for reconsideration, request for review, application for reexamination, referral for application for compulsory medical treatment, and referral for application for confiscation of illegal gains;
(2) Cases referred by supervisory organs for prosecution, request for confiscation of illegal gains, and request for reconsideration of a decision not to prosecute;
(3) Cases where a lower-level People's Procuratorate raises or submits a protest, requests designation of jurisdiction, requests approval of prosecution, requests approval of trial in absentia, or requests supervision of review of death penalty;
(4) Cases where a People's Court notifies attendance at a court of second instance or a retrial court;
(5) Other cases that shall be accepted by the case management department according to the provisions.

Article 157 When accepting a case, the case management department of a People's Procuratorate shall receive the case file materials and immediately examine the following:
(1) Determine whether the case falls within the jurisdiction of this procuratorate based on the contents specified in the transferred legal documents;
(2) Whether the case file materials are complete, standardized, and meet the requirements of relevant provisions;
(3) Whether the transferred funds or articles match the transfer list;
(4) Whether the criminal suspect is present and the status of compulsory measures taken;
(5) Whether the case is transferred within the specified time limit.

Article 161 The department responsible for procuratorial work on complaints and appeals of a People's Procuratorate shall uniformly accept reports of crimes, complaints, information, petitions and voluntary surrender of criminal suspects, examine them according to law, and make the following dispositions within seven days:
(1) If the matter falls within the jurisdiction of this procuratorate and meets the conditions for acceptance, it shall be accepted;
(2) If the report, complaint, information, or voluntary surrender does not fall within the jurisdiction of this procuratorate, it shall be transferred to the competent organ for handling. If emergency measures must be taken, it shall take emergency measures first and then transfer the matter to the competent organ. If the petition does not fall within the jurisdiction of this procuratorate, the petitioner shall be informed to submit it to the organ with jurisdiction;
(3) If the case circumstances are unclear, necessary investigation and verification shall be conducted, and a disposition shall be made according to law after the circumstances are ascertained.
The department responsible for procuratorial work on complaints and appeals may assign cases of complaints, appeals and information to lower-level People's Procuratorates and supervise their handling in accordance with relevant provisions.

Article 162 A People's Procuratorate shall accept a complaint or petition if the following conditions are met:
(1) It falls within the scope of cases accepted by the People's Procuratorate;
(2) This procuratorate has jurisdiction;
(3) The petitioner is a party to the original case or his or her legal representative or near relative;
(4) The complaint or petition materials meet the acceptance requirements.
If the complainant or petitioner entrusts a lawyer to act as agent for the complaint or petition, and the above conditions are met, it shall be accepted.
If the complaint or petition materials are incomplete, the complainant or petitioner shall be informed to supplement them. The acceptance time shall be calculated from the date when the complainant or petitioner supplements the relevant materials.

Article 205 When conducting a search, a search warrant shall be presented to the person to be searched or their family members.
When executing an arrest or detention, if any of the following emergency situations arise, a search may be conducted without a separate search warrant:
(1) The person may be carrying a weapon;
(2) The person may be hiding dangerous articles such as explosives or highly toxic substances;
(3) The person may be concealing, destroying, or transferring criminal evidence;
(4) The person may be hiding another criminal suspect;
(5) Other emergency situations.
After the search is completed, the search personnel shall complete the relevant formalities within twenty-four hours.

Article 242 If, during or after the investigation, a People's Procuratorate discovers that any of the following circumstances exist, the department responsible for investigation shall prepare a draft opinion on dismissing the case and submit it to the chief procurator for decision:
(1) Any of the circumstances provided in Article 16 of the Criminal Procedure Law exist;
(2) There is no criminal fact, or according to the Criminal Law, the person does not bear criminal liability or the act does not constitute a crime;
(3) Although a criminal fact exists, it was not committed by the criminal suspect.
For a case of joint crime, if there is a criminal suspect who meets the circumstances provided in this Article, the filing of the case against that criminal suspect shall be dismissed.

Article 269: If the criminal suspect pleads guilty and accepts punishment, a People's Procuratorate shall inform him of the litigation rights he enjoys and the legal provisions on pleading guilty and accepting punishment, and shall hear the opinions of the criminal suspect, the defender or duty lawyer, the victim, and his legal representative on the following matters, and record them in the case file:
(1) The suspected criminal facts, charges, and applicable legal provisions;
(2) Recommendations for lenient punishment such as lighter, mitigated, or exempted punishment;
(3) The procedures applicable to the trial of the case after pleading guilty and accepting punishment;
(4) Other matters on which opinions need to be heard.
If the opinions of the duty lawyer are to be heard in accordance with the preceding paragraph, necessary convenience shall be provided in advance for the duty lawyer to understand the relevant circumstances of the case. From the date a People's Procuratorate begins to review the case for prosecution, the duty lawyer may consult the case file materials to understand the case. A People's Procuratorate shall facilitate the duty lawyer's access to the case file materials.
If a People's Procuratorate does not adopt the opinions raised by the defender or duty lawyer, it shall explain the reasons therefor to them.

Article 271 During the stage of examination and prosecution, for a case in which the criminal suspect has admitted guilt and accepted punishment during the investigation stage, the People's Procuratorate shall focus on examining the following:
(1) Whether the criminal suspect has voluntarily admitted guilt and accepted punishment, and whether there has been any violation of his or her will to admit guilt and accept punishment due to violence, threats, or inducements;
(2) Whether the criminal suspect's cognitive ability and mental state were normal at the time of admitting guilt and accepting punishment;
(3) Whether the criminal suspect understands the nature of admitting guilt and accepting punishment and the possible legal consequences;
(4) Whether the public security organ has informed the criminal suspect of the litigation rights he or she enjoys, the legal provisions that truthful confession of one's own crimes may lead to lenient treatment and that admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment are legal, and has heard opinions;
(5) Whether the opinion on prosecution specifies the criminal suspect's admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment;
(6) Whether the criminal suspect sincerely repents and whether he or she has apologized to the victim.
Upon examination, if the criminal suspect has admitted guilt and accepted punishment against his or her will, the People's Procuratorate may re-conduct the work of admitting guilt and accepting punishment. If there are illegal evidence collection acts such as extorting confessions by torture, the matter shall be handled in accordance with law.

Article 272 Where a criminal suspect voluntarily admits guilt and accepts punishment and agrees to the sentencing recommendation and the application of the procedure, he or she shall sign a written statement of admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment in the presence of a defender or a duty lawyer. The written statement shall include the criminal suspect's truthful confession of the offense, agreement to the sentencing recommendation and the application of the procedure, and shall be signed by the criminal suspect and his or her defender or the duty lawyer.
A criminal suspect need not sign a written statement of admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The criminal suspect is blind, deaf, or mute, or is a person with a mental illness who has not completely lost the ability to recognize or control his or her conduct;
(2) The legal representative or defender of a minor criminal suspect objects to the minor's admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment;
(3) Other circumstances in which it is unnecessary to sign a written statement of admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment.
In the circumstances of the preceding paragraph, the criminal suspect's failure to sign a written statement of admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment shall not affect the application of the system of leniency for admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment.

Article 278 Where, after the People's Procuratorate makes a decision not to prosecute in accordance with paragraph 2 of Article 177 of the Criminal Procedure Law on the ground that the criminal suspect admits guilt and accepts punishment, the criminal suspect withdraws his or her admission, the People's Procuratorate shall conduct a review and handle the matter according to law by distinguishing the following circumstances:
(1) If it is found that the criminal suspect has no criminal facts or falls under one of the circumstances provided for in Article 16 of the Criminal Procedure Law, it shall revoke the original decision not to prosecute and make a new decision not to prosecute in accordance with paragraph 1 of Article 177 of the Criminal Procedure Law;
(2) If the criminal suspect's crime is minor and no punishment is needed or criminal punishment may be exempted according to the Criminal Law, the original decision not to prosecute may be maintained;
(3) If, after excluding the factor of admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment, the conditions for prosecution are met, it shall revoke the original decision not to prosecute according to the specific circumstances of the case and initiate public prosecution according to law.

Article 280 When handling a case of examination for approval of arrest, the People's Procuratorate may interrogate the criminal suspect; under any of the following circumstances, it shall interrogate the criminal suspect:
(1) There is doubt as to whether the conditions for arrest are met;
(2) The criminal suspect requests to make a statement in person to the procuratorial personnel;
(3) The investigation activities may involve major illegal acts;
(4) The case is major, difficult, or complex;
(5) The criminal suspect admits guilt and accepts punishment;
(6) The criminal suspect is a minor;
(7) The criminal suspect is blind, deaf, or mute, or is a person with a mental illness who has not completely lost the ability to recognize or control his or her conduct.
If the criminal suspect not in custody is to be interrogated, the opinions of the public security organ shall be heard before interrogation.
When handling a case of examination for approval of arrest, if a criminal suspect in custody is not interrogated, a form for hearing the criminal suspect's opinion shall be served, and after being filled out by the criminal suspect, it shall be promptly retrieved, examined, and attached to the case file. If, upon examination, it is considered necessary to interrogate the criminal suspect, the interrogation shall be carried out promptly.

Article 311 For a case that concurrently meets the following conditions, the People's Procuratorate shall make a decision to approve the extension of the period of custodial investigation by one month:
(1) It meets the provisions of Article 156 of the Criminal Procedure Law;
(2) It meets the conditions for arrest;
(3) There is a need for continued custody of the criminal suspect.

Article 322 A case reported for approval of prosecution shall concurrently meet the following conditions:
(1) There is evidence proving the existence of criminal facts and that the criminal facts were committed by the criminal suspect;
(2) The maximum statutory punishment for the sentencing range applicable to the suspected criminal conduct is life imprisonment or the death penalty;
(3) The nature, circumstances, and consequences of the suspected crime are particularly serious. Although the twenty-year limitation period for prosecution has expired, the social harmfulness and impact still exist, and failure to prosecute would seriously affect social stability or cause other serious consequences, making prosecution necessary;
(4) The criminal suspect is able to appear for prosecution in a timely manner.

Article 330 When reviewing a case transferred for prosecution, the People's Procuratorate shall ascertain:
(1) Whether the identity status of the criminal suspect is clear, including name, gender, nationality, date of birth, occupation, and work unit; if it is a unit crime, whether the relevant information of the unit is clear;
(2) Whether the criminal facts and circumstances are clear; whether the time, place, means, and harmful consequences of the crime are definite;
(3) Whether the opinion on the nature of the crime and the charge is correct; whether there are statutory circumstances for heavier, lighter, mitigated, or exempted punishment and discretionary circumstances for heavier or lighter punishment; whether the determination of responsibility of the criminal suspect in the criminal activities in a joint crime case is appropriate;
(4) Whether the criminal suspect admits guilt and accepts punishment;
(5) Whether the evidentiary materials proving the criminal facts are transferred with the case; whether the evidentiary materials proving that the relevant property is illegal gains are transferred with the case; whether the list, copies, photographs, or other certification documents of evidence that are not suitable for transfer are transferred with the case;
(6) Whether the evidence is reliable and sufficient, whether it was collected according to law, and whether there are circumstances where illegal evidence should be excluded;
(7) Whether the legal formalities and litigation documents for the investigation measures, including technical investigation measures, are complete;
(8) Whether there are any omitted crimes or other persons who should be investigated for criminal liability;
(9) Whether the case is one where criminal liability should not be investigated;
(10) Whether there is an incidental civil action; if state property or collective property has suffered losses, whether it is necessary for the People's Procuratorate to initiate an incidental civil action; for acts that damage the ecological environment and resource protection, infringe upon the lawful rights and interests of many consumers in the fields of food and drug safety, or harm the name, portrait, reputation, honor, etc., of heroes and martyrs, thereby harming social public interests, whether it is necessary for the People's Procuratorate to initiate an incidental civil public interest action;
(11) Whether the compulsory measures taken are appropriate; for a criminal suspect who has already been arrested, whether there is a need for continued custody;
(12) Whether the investigation activities are lawful;
(13) Whether the property involved in the case has been sealed, seized, frozen, and properly preserved, and whether the list is complete; whether the return of the lawful property of the victim and the disposition of prohibited goods or items not suitable for long-term preservation are appropriate, and whether the transferred certification documents are complete.

Article 344 For a case transferred by a supervisory organ for prosecution, under any of the following circumstances, a People's Procuratorate may conduct supplementary investigation on its own:
(1) The main facts in the witness testimony, the criminal suspect's confession and defense, and the victim's statement are consistent, but individual details are inconsistent;
(2) Physical evidence, documentary evidence, and other evidentiary materials need supplementary expert evaluation;
(3) Other circumstances where it is more convenient, more efficient, or more conducive to ascertaining the facts of the case for the People's Procuratorate to investigate.
After the supplementary investigation on its own is completed, the relevant evidentiary materials shall be placed in the case file, and a copy shall be sent to the supervisory organ. Where a People's Procuratorate conducts supplementary investigation on its own, it may consult with the supervisory organ to provide assistance.

Article 355: If a People's Procuratorate finds that the criminal facts of a criminal suspect have been established, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, and criminal liability shall be investigated according to law, it shall make a decision to initiate a prosecution.
Under any of the following circumstances, the criminal facts may be deemed to have been established:
(1) In a case involving a single crime, the established facts are sufficient for conviction and sentencing, or the facts relevant to conviction and sentencing have been established, while facts that do not affect conviction and sentencing cannot be established;
(2) In a case involving multiple crimes, some crimes have been established and meet the conditions for prosecution, while other crimes cannot be established;
(3) The whereabouts of the tools used in the crime or the stolen goods cannot be established, but other evidence is sufficient to convict and sentence the defendant;
(4) The main facts stated in witness testimony, the criminal suspect's confession and defense, and the victim's statement are consistent, while individual details are inconsistent, but such inconsistency does not affect the conviction.
For cases that fall under item (2) of the preceding paragraph, the prosecution shall be initiated based on the crimes that have been established.

Article 358: If a People's Procuratorate decides to initiate a prosecution, it shall prepare an indictment.
The main contents of the indictment include:
(1) The basic information of the defendant, including name, gender, date of birth, place of birth and registered domicile, citizen identification number, ethnicity, education level, occupation, work unit and position, address, whether any criminal punishment has been imposed and the type and time of such punishment, and the status of compulsory measures taken; if it is a unit crime, the name and organization code of the criminal unit, its address and contact information, and the names, positions, and contact information of the legal representative and litigation representative shall be stated; if there are directly responsible supervisors or other directly responsible personnel who shall bear criminal liability, they shall be described in accordance with the content of the basic information of the defendant above;
(2) The cause of action and the source of the case;
(3) The facts of the case, including the time, place, process, means, motive, purpose, harmful consequences, and other factual elements relevant to conviction and sentencing. The essential elements of the alleged criminal facts described in the indictment shall be clear and accurate. If the defendant is accused of multiple criminal facts, they shall be listed one by one; for the same crime with identical means, a summary description may be used;
(4) The basis and reasons for the prosecution, including the provisions of the Criminal Law violated by the defendant, the nature of the crime and the identified charge, the punishment provisions, the statutory circumstances for lighter, mitigated, or heavier punishment, and the criminal responsibility of each defendant in a joint crime;
(5) The defendant's admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment, including the content of the admission and acceptance, and the signing of the recognizance letter.
If the true name and address of the defendant cannot be ascertained, the indictment may be prepared based on his alias or the name and address he reports, and such fact shall be noted in the indictment. If the name reported by the defendant may cause adverse effects such as harming the reputation of others or corrupting moral customs, the defendant may be assigned a number and the indictment prepared based on that number, with a photograph of the defendant attached, and a record of matters sufficient to identify the defendant's appearance, physique, fingerprints, and other characteristics.
The indictment shall be accompanied by the defendant's current location, a list of witnesses, expert evaluators, and persons with specialized knowledge who need to appear in court, a pseudonym list of victims, witnesses, and expert evaluators who need protection, a list of property and its fruits that have been sealed up, seized, or frozen, information on incidental civil actions and incidental civil public interest litigation, and other matters requiring annotation.
The list of witnesses, expert evaluators, and persons with specialized knowledge shall include their names, genders, ages, occupations, addresses, and contact information, and shall indicate whether the witnesses and expert evaluators will appear in court.

Article 368: Under any of the following circumstances, if it cannot be determined that a criminal suspect has committed a crime and that criminal liability shall be investigated, the evidence is deemed insufficient and does not meet the conditions for prosecution:
(1) The facts of the constitutive elements of the crime lack necessary evidence to prove them;
(2) The evidence on which the conviction is based is questionable and cannot be verified as true;
(3) Contradictions among the evidence on which the conviction is based, or between the evidence and the facts of the case, cannot be reasonably resolved;
(4) The conclusion drawn from the evidence has other possibilities, and reasonable doubt cannot be eliminated;
(5) The determination of the facts of the case based on the evidence is inconsistent with logic and the rules of experience, and the conclusion reached is obviously contrary to common sense.

Article 372 Where a People's Procuratorate decides not to prosecute, it shall make a written decision not to prosecute.
The main contents of the written decision not to prosecute include:
(1) The basic information of the person not prosecuted, including name, gender, date of birth, place of birth and registered domicile, citizen identity number, ethnicity, education level, occupation, work unit and position, address, whether previously subjected to criminal punishment, compulsory measures taken, and place of custody; if it is a unit crime, the name and organization code of the criminal unit, its address, contact information, and the name, position, and contact information of its legal representative and litigation representative shall be stated;
(2) The cause of action and source of the case;
(3) The facts of the case, including facts that negate or allege that the person not prosecuted constitutes a crime and the facts serving as the basis for the decision not to prosecute;
(4) The legal basis and reasons for non-prosecution, stating the applicable legal provisions for the decision not to prosecute;
(5) The handling of the property involved that has been sealed, seized, or frozen;
(6) Relevant matters to be notified.

Article 392 Where the People's Court decides to open a court session for trial, the public prosecutor shall make the following preparations:
(1) Further familiarize himself with the case facts and master the evidence situation;
(2) Conduct in-depth research on legal and policy issues related to the case;
(3) Enrich the professional knowledge that may be involved in the trial;
(4) Formulate plans for interrogating the defendant, questioning witnesses, expert evaluators, and persons with specialized knowledge, and for reading, presenting, and playing evidence, and develop a cross-examination plan;
(5) Where disputes over the legality of evidence may arise, formulate an outline for proving the legality of evidence and prepare relevant materials;
(6) Formulate public prosecution opinions and prepare a debate outline;
(7) Where it is necessary to make recommendations to the People's Court or cooperate in protecting witnesses appearing in court, make relevant preparations.

Article 398 The public prosecutor shall, in court, carry out the following activities according to law:
(1) Read the indictment, accuse the crime on behalf of the state, and request the People's Court to try the defendant according to law;
(2) Interrogate the defendant;
(3) Question witnesses, victims, and expert evaluators;
(4) Apply to the court to present physical evidence, read documentary evidence, written testimony of witnesses not appearing in court, expert evaluation opinions, records of inquests, inspections, identifications, investigative experiments, and other documents as evidence, and play audio-visual materials, electronic data, etc., as evidence;
(5) Express opinions on the admissibility of evidence, application of law, and the circumstances of the case, propose sentencing recommendations and reasons, respond to the defense opinions of the defendant and defender, and comprehensively elaborate the public prosecution opinions;
(6) Protect the lawful rights of participants in the proceedings;
(7) Record in the minutes whether the court's trial of the case violates legal procedures;
(8) Engage in other litigation activities according to law.

Article 400 When interrogating the defendant, questioning witnesses, victims, and expert evaluators, presenting physical evidence, reading documentary evidence, written testimony of witnesses not appearing in court, etc., the public prosecutor shall focus on the following facts:
(1) The identity of the defendant;
(2) Whether the alleged criminal facts exist and whether they were committed by the defendant;
(3) The time, place, method, means, and result of the commission of the criminal act, and the conduct of the defendant after the crime;
(4) The respective status and liability of persons participating in the crime in a criminal group or other joint crime cases;
(5) Whether the defendant has the capacity for criminal liability, whether there is intent or negligence, and the motive and purpose of the act;
(6) Whether there are circumstances under which criminal liability should not be investigated according to law, and whether there are legally prescribed circumstances for aggravated, mitigated, reduced, or exempted punishment;
(7) The main characteristics of the object of crime and the tools used, and the source, quantity, and destination of property related to the crime;
(8) If the defendant denies in whole or in part the criminal facts alleged in the indictment, whether the grounds and reasons for the denial are tenable;
(9) Other facts related to conviction and sentencing.

Article 401 In court trial, the following facts need not be proved by evidence:
(1) Common knowledge facts generally known to the public;
(2) Facts confirmed by a legally effective judgment of the People's Court and not retried under the trial supervision procedure;
(3) Facts that the content and application of laws and regulations, etc., fall within the scope that trial personnel should know in performing their duties;
(4) Procedural facts in court trial to which no objection exists;
(5) Facts presumed by law;
(6) Laws of nature or established principles.

Article 420 During court adjudication, in any of the following circumstances, the public prosecutor may suggest that the court adjourn the hearing:
(1) Where facts are unclear or evidence is insufficient, or there is an omission of crimes or of co-suspects, and supplementary investigation or supplementary provision of evidence is necessary;
(2) Where the defendant reveals another person's criminal act or provides important leads, requiring supplementary investigation for verification;
(3) Where an omission of crimes or co-suspects is discovered, and although supplementary investigation or supplementary provision of evidence is not necessary, supplementary or additional prosecution is needed;
(4) Where a request is made for the People's Court to notify witnesses, expert evaluators to testify in court, or experts to appear in court to provide opinions;
(5) Where it is necessary to obtain new evidence, conduct a new expert evaluation or inspection;
(6) Where the public prosecutor presents or reads out evidence not among the evidence transferred to the People's Court before trial, or supplements, adds to, or modifies the prosecution, requiring that the defendant and his defender be given necessary time to prepare their defense;
(7) Where the defendant or his defender presents to the court evidence related to conviction and sentencing that is not in the possession of the public prosecutor and requires investigation and verification;
(8) Where the public prosecutor proves the legality of evidence collection and requires investigation and verification.
If any of the circumstances in the preceding paragraph is discovered before the People's Court opens the trial, the People's Procuratorate may suggest that the People's Court adjourn the hearing.

Article 424 Before the People's Court pronounces judgment, if the People's Procuratorate discovers any of the following circumstances, with the approval of the chief procurator, it may withdraw the prosecution:
(1) There is no criminal fact;
(2) The criminal fact was not committed by the defendant;
(3) The circumstances are obviously minor, the harm done is not significant, and it is not considered a crime;
(4) The evidence is insufficient or has changed, and the conditions for prosecution are not met;
(5) The defendant does not bear criminal liability because he has not reached the age of criminal liability;
(6) Changes in laws or judicial interpretations result in the defendant no longer being subject to criminal liability;
(7) Other circumstances where the defendant should not be subject to criminal liability.
For a case where the prosecution is withdrawn, the People's Procuratorate shall make a decision not to prosecute within thirty days after the withdrawal. If reinvestigation or supplementary investigation is necessary, after making the decision not to prosecute, the case file materials shall be returned to the supervisory organ or the public security organ, and the People's Procuratorate shall suggest that the supervisory organ or the public security organ reinvestigate or conduct supplementary investigation, and state the reasons in writing.
For a case where the prosecution is withdrawn, the People's Procuratorate shall not again initiate prosecution unless there are new facts or new evidence.
New facts refer to criminal facts not alleged in the original indictment. The crime implicated by such criminal facts may be the same as the originally charged crime or a different crime.
New evidence refers to evidence collected or produced after the withdrawal that is sufficient to prove the originally alleged criminal facts.

Article 430 For cases under the jurisdiction of a Primary People's Court, if the following conditions are met, the People's Procuratorate may suggest that the People's Court apply summary procedure:
(1) The facts of the case are clear and the evidence is sufficient;
(2) The defendant admits the crime he committed and does not object to the alleged criminal facts;
(3) The defendant does not object to the application of summary procedure.

Article 431 In any of the following circumstances, the People's Procuratorate shall not recommend that the People's Court apply the summary procedure:
(1) The defendant is blind, deaf or mute, or is a mental patient who has not completely lost the ability to recognize or control his own conduct;
(2) The case has a significant social impact;
(3) In a joint crime case, some defendants do not plead guilty or object to the application of the summary procedure;
(4) The joint crime case is relatively complex;
(5) The defender makes a defense of innocence or objects to the principal criminal facts;
(6) Other circumstances where the summary procedure is not appropriate.
If the People's Court decides to try a case by applying the summary procedure, and the People's Procuratorate deems that the case falls under any of the circumstances provided for in Article 215 of the Criminal Procedure Law, it shall submit corrective opinions to the People's Court; if there are other circumstances where the summary procedure is not appropriate, the People's Procuratorate may recommend that the People's Court not apply the summary procedure.

Article 437 Where a case under the jurisdiction of a Primary People's Court meets the following conditions, the People's Procuratorate may, when initiating a public prosecution, recommend that the People's Court try the case by applying the fast-track sentencing procedure:
(1) The defendant may be sentenced to a punishment of fixed-term imprisonment of not more than three years;
(2) The facts of the case are clear, and the evidence is reliable and sufficient;
(3) The defendant pleads guilty and accepts punishment, and agrees to the application of the fast-track sentencing procedure.

Article 438 In any of the following circumstances, the People's Procuratorate shall not recommend that the People's Court apply the fast-track sentencing procedure:
(1) The defendant is blind, deaf or mute, or is a mental patient who has not completely lost the ability to recognize or control his own conduct;
(2) The defendant is a minor;
(3) The case has a significant social impact;
(4) In a joint crime case, some defendants object to the charged criminal facts, the charges, the sentencing recommendation, or the application of the fast-track sentencing procedure;
(5) The defendant and the victim or his statutory agent have not reached a mediation or settlement agreement on matters such as compensation in the incidental civil action;
(6) Other circumstances where the fast-track sentencing procedure is not appropriate to be applied.

Article 446 The tasks of a prosecutor appearing before the court of second instance are:
(1) To support the protest or to hear the opinions on appeal, and to submit corrective opinions on the erroneous judgment or ruling made by the original trial People's Court;
(2) To uphold the correct judgment or ruling of the original trial People's Court, and to recommend that the court affirm the original judgment;
(3) To protect the lawful rights of participants in the proceedings;
(4) To record in the transcript whether the court's trial violates the litigation procedures provided by law;
(5) To engage in other litigation activities according to law.

Article 449 When reviewing the case file materials of the first instance, the procuratorial personnel shall verify the main evidence and may interrogate the original trial defendant. When necessary, they may supplement the collection of evidence, conduct a new expert evaluation, or supplement an expert evaluation. If the public security organ that originally investigated the case needs to supplement the collection of evidence, they may request such supplementation.
If the defendant or the defender submits materials or clues concerning the defendant's voluntary surrender, meritorious service, or other matters that may affect conviction and sentencing, such materials and clues may be handed over to the public security organ for investigation and verification, or they may be investigated and verified by the procuratorial personnel themselves. If omitted crimes or joint crime suspects are discovered, the procuratorial personnel shall recommend that the public security organ investigate.
The following original trial defendants shall be interrogated:
(1) Those who file an appeal;
(2) Those against whom the People's Procuratorate files a protest;
(3) Those who have been sentenced to a punishment of life imprisonment or above.

Article 450 When handling a death penalty appeal or protest case, the People's Procuratorate shall carry out the following work:
(1) Interrogate the original trial defendant and hear the reasons for his appeal or his defense;
(2) Hear the defender's opinions;
(3) Verify the main evidence and, when necessary, question witnesses;
(4) Supplement the collection of evidence when necessary;
(5) If there is doubt about the expert opinion, a new expert evaluation may be conducted or an expert evaluation may be supplemented;
(6) Depending on the circumstances of the case, the victim's opinions may be heard.

Article 463 Where a minor criminal suspect has committed a relatively minor crime, has effective guardianship conditions or community assistance and education measures, and has no social dangerousness or has relatively low social dangerousness, the People's Procuratorate shall disapprove the arrest.
Where a minor criminal suspect has committed a relatively serious crime, but his or her subjective malignancy is not high, shows signs of repentance, has effective guardianship conditions or community assistance and education measures, and falls under any of the following circumstances, and non-arrest would not create social dangerousness, the People's Procuratorate may disapprove the arrest:
(1) first offense or negligent offense;
(2) preparation for a crime, discontinuation of a crime, or attempted crime;
(3) excessive defense or excessive necessity;
(4) voluntary surrender or meritorious performance;
(5) after committing the crime, admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment, or active return of illegal gains, doing the utmost to reduce and compensate for losses, and the victim has provided understanding;
(6) not being a principal offender in a joint crime or a ringleader in a gang crime;
(7) being a minor who has reached the age of 14 but not the age of 16, or being a student;
(8) other circumstances where arrest may be disapproved.
Where release on bail pending investigation or trial is applied to a minor criminal suspect who does not have a fixed domicile and cannot provide a guarantor, a suitable adult may be designated as the guarantor.

Article 475 The People's Procuratorate shall supervise and review whether the juvenile criminal suspect who is subject to conditional non-prosecution complies with the following provisions:
(1) Comply with laws and regulations and submit to supervision;
(2) Report his or her activities in accordance with the provisions;
(3) Obtain approval before leaving the city or county where he or she resides or changing residence;
(4) Receive correction and education as required.

Article 476 The People's Procuratorate may require the juvenile criminal suspect who is subject to conditional non-prosecution to receive the following correction and education:
(1) Complete addiction treatment, psychological counseling, or other appropriate treatment measures;
(2) Provide community service or volunteer work for the community or public welfare organizations;
(3) Not enter specific places, meet or communicate with specific persons, or engage in specific activities;
(4) Compensate the victim for losses, apologize, etc.;
(5) Receive relevant education;
(6) Comply with other prohibitive provisions to protect the safety of the victim and prevent recidivism.

Article 479 Where the juvenile criminal suspect who is subject to conditional non-prosecution falls under any of the following circumstances during the probation period, the People's Procuratorate shall revoke the decision on conditional non-prosecution and initiate a public prosecution:
(1) Commits a new crime;
(2) It is discovered that there are other crimes that need to be prosecuted before the decision on conditional non-prosecution;
(3) Violates public security administration provisions, causing serious consequences, or repeatedly violates public security administration provisions;
(4) Violates the supervision and management provisions on conditional non-prosecution, causing serious consequences, or repeatedly violates the supervision and management provisions on conditional non-prosecution.

Article 497: The People's Procuratorate shall examine the voluntariness and legality of the settlement, focusing on the following contents:
(1) whether the parties voluntarily entered into the settlement;
(2) whether the criminal suspect sincerely repents, has made an apology to the victim, and whether the amount of compensation is commensurate with the harm caused and the ability to compensate;
(3) whether the victim, his or her legal representative or close relative expressly indicates that he or she has forgiven the criminal suspect;
(4) whether it complies with the legal provisions;
(5) whether it damages the national, collective or social public interests or the lawful rights and interests of others; and
(6) whether it conforms to social morality.
When conducting the examination, opinions on the settlement shall be heard from the parties and other relevant persons, the legal consequences of possible lenient handling in criminal cases and the rights and obligations of both parties shall be informed, and records shall be made and attached to the case file.

Article 498: If, after examination, it is believed that both parties voluntarily settle, the content is lawful, and it meets the scope and conditions specified in Article 492 of these Rules, the People's Procuratorate shall preside over the preparation of a settlement agreement.
The main contents of the settlement agreement include:
(1) basic information of the parties;
(2) the main facts of the case;
(3) the criminal suspect sincerely repents, admits the crime he or she committed, has no objection to the charged crime, and provides compensation for losses, makes an apology, etc. to the victim. For compensation for losses, the amount of compensation, the method and time limit for performance, etc. shall be specified;
(4) the victim, his or her legal representative or close relative has forgiven the criminal suspect and requests or agrees that the public security organ, the People's Procuratorate, or the People's Court shall handle the criminal suspect leniently according to law.
The settlement agreement shall be signed by the parties, and it may be stated that the settlement agreement was prepared under the auspices of the People's Procuratorate. The procuratorial personnel shall not sign the settlement agreement, nor shall the seal of the People's Procuratorate be affixed.
The settlement agreement shall be made in triplicate, with one copy held by each party and the third copy submitted to the People's Procuratorate for attachment to the case file for reference.

Article 520 Where the People's Procuratorate files an application with the People's Court for confiscation of illegal gains, it shall prepare a written application for confiscation of illegal gains. The written application for confiscation of illegal gains shall specify the following:
(1) The basic information of the criminal suspect or defendant, including name, sex, date of birth, place of birth, place of household registration, citizen identity number, ethnicity, education level, occupation, work unit and position, address, etc.;
(2) The cause of action and source of the case;
(3) The facts of the crime committed by the criminal suspect or defendant and relevant evidence materials;
(4) The circumstances of the criminal suspect or defendant absconding, being wanted, or dying;
(5) The type, quantity, value, and location of the property for which confiscation is applied, as well as the list of sealed, seized, or frozen property and relevant legal formalities;
(6) The relevant facts and evidence materials that the property for which confiscation is applied constitutes illegal gains or other property involved in the case;
(7) The reasons and legal basis for filing the application for confiscation of illegal gains;
(8) Whether there are close relatives or other interested parties, and the names, identities, addresses, and contact information of the interested parties;
(9) Other content that shall be specified.
If the above materials require translation, the People's Procuratorate shall transfer them to the People's Court together with the written application for confiscation of illegal gains.

Article 522 When reviewing the opinion on confiscation of illegal gains transferred by a supervisory organ or public security organ, the People's Procuratorate shall review the following:
(1) Whether it falls under the jurisdiction of this People's Procuratorate;
(2) Whether the conditions provided in paragraph 1 of Article 298 of the Criminal Procedure Law are met;
(3) The basic information of the criminal suspect, including name, sex, nationality, date of birth, occupation, and work unit;
(4) The facts of the crime suspected by the criminal suspect and relevant evidence materials;
(5) The circumstances of the criminal suspect absconding, missing, being wanted, or dying, and whether the wanted notice or death certificate is transferred with the case;
(6) The type, quantity, and location of illegal gains and other property involved in the case, and the circumstances of sealing, seizure, and freezing, and whether the list of sealed, seized, or frozen property and relevant legal formalities are transferred with the case;
(7) The relevant facts and evidence materials concerning the illegal gains and other property involved in the case;
(8) Whether there are close relatives and other interested parties, and the names, identities, addresses, and contact information of the interested parties.
If it is inappropriate to transfer the evidence materials related to the facts of the crime, illegal gains, and other property involved in the case, it shall be reviewed whether a list of evidence, copies, photographs, or other certifying documents are transferred with the case.

Article 536 Where a People's Procuratorate submits an application for compulsory medical treatment to the People's Court, it shall prepare a written application for compulsory medical treatment. The main contents of the written application for compulsory medical treatment shall include:
(1) The basic information of the mental patient involved in the case, including name, sex, date of birth, place of birth, registered permanent residence, citizen identity number, ethnicity, education level, occupation, work unit and position, address, the circumstances and location of temporary protective restraint measures, etc.;
(2) The basic information of the legal representative of the mental patient involved in the case, including name, address, contact information, etc.;
(3) The cause of action and the source of the case;
(4) The facts of the violent acts committed by the mental patient involved in the case that endangered public security or seriously endangered the personal safety of citizens, including the time, place, means, consequences, etc. of the violent acts and relevant evidence;
(5) The basis for the mental patient involved in the case being exempt from criminal liability, including relevant expert opinions and other evidentiary materials;
(6) The possibility that the mental patient involved in the case may continue to endanger society;
(7) The reasons and legal basis for applying for compulsory medical treatment.

Article 537 When reviewing the written opinion on compulsory medical treatment transferred by a public security organ, the People's Procuratorate shall ascertain:
(1) Whether the case falls within its own jurisdiction;
(2) Whether the identity status of the mental patient involved in the case is clear, including name, sex, nationality, date of birth, occupation, and work unit, etc.;
(3) The facts of the violent acts committed by the mental patient involved in the case that endangered public security or seriously endangered the personal safety of citizens;
(4) Whether the procedures for the public security organ's evaluation of the mental patient involved in the case are lawful, and whether the mental patient involved in the case is exempt from criminal liability according to law;
(5) Whether the mental patient involved in the case may continue to endanger society;
(6) Whether the evidentiary materials are transferred together with the case, and whether the list, copies, photographs or other supporting documents of evidence not suitable for transfer are transferred together with the case;
(7) Whether the evidence is truthful and sufficient;
(8) Whether the temporary protective restraint measures taken are appropriate.

Article 538 When handling a compulsory medical treatment case transferred by a public security organ, the People's Procuratorate may conduct investigations by the following methods, and the investigation circumstances shall be recorded and attached to the case file:
(1) Meeting the mental patient involved in the case and hearing the opinions of the legal representative and litigation agent of the mental patient involved in the case;
(2) Questioning the case-handling personnel and expert evaluators;
(3) Inquiring about the circumstances from the victim and the victim's legal representative and close relatives;
(4) Inquiring about the circumstances from the attending physician, close relatives, neighbors, other knowledgeable persons, or grassroots organizations of the mental patient involved in the case;
(5) Commissioning an evaluation institution or expert evaluator with legally prescribed qualifications to conduct an evaluation on relevant specialized technical issues.

Article 541 Where a People's Procuratorate, in a compulsory medical treatment case transferred by a public security organ, discovers that the public security organ's evaluation of the mental patient involved in the case violates legal provisions and falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall submit a corrective opinion according to law:
(1) The evaluation institution does not possess the legally prescribed qualifications;
(2) The evaluator does not possess the legally prescribed qualifications or violates the recusal provisions;
(3) The evaluation procedure violates the law or relevant provisions, and the process and methods of evaluation violate relevant professional standards;
(4) The evaluation document does not meet the legally prescribed formal requirements;
(5) The evaluation opinion was not timely informed to the relevant persons according to law;
(6) The evaluator intentionally makes a false evaluation;
(7) Other circumstances that violate legal provisions.
When supervising the psychiatric evaluation procedure, the People's Procuratorate may request the public security organ to supplement or conduct a new evaluation. If necessary, it may question the evaluator and make a record, or commission an evaluation institution with legally prescribed qualifications to conduct a supplementary or new evaluation.

Article 545 Where a People's Procuratorate discovers that the People's Court's adjudication activities in a compulsory medical treatment case fall under any of the following circumstances, it shall submit a corrective opinion:
(1) Failure to notify the legal representative of the respondent or the defendant to be present;
(2) Where the respondent or the defendant has not appointed a litigation agent, failure to notify a legal aid institution to assign a lawyer to provide legal assistance;
(3) Failure to form a collegial panel or the composition of the collegial panel is unlawful;
(4) Making a decision not to hold a court hearing without a request from the legal representative of the respondent or the defendant;
(5) Failure to meet the respondent;
(6) Where the respondent or the defendant requests to appear in court and meets the conditions for appearing in court, failure to permit their appearance;
(7) Violation of the statutory adjudication time limit;
(8) After receiving written corrective opinions from the People's Procuratorate regarding an improper compulsory medical treatment decision, failure to form a new collegial panel for adjudication or failure to make a reconsideration decision within one month;
(9) The People's Court's decision on compulsory medical treatment or decision to reject the application for compulsory medical treatment is improper;
(10) Other circumstances that violate legal provisions.

Article 547 Where a People's Procuratorate considers that a People's Court's decision on compulsory medical treatment or decision to reject an application for compulsory medical treatment falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall submit a corrective opinion to the People's Court within twenty days after receiving the copy of the decision:
(1) The facts on which the decision is based are unclear or indeed erroneous;
(2) The evidence on which the decision is based is not truthful or insufficient;
(3) The evidence on which the decision is based should be excluded according to law;
(4) There are contradictions among the main evidence on which the decision is based;
(5) There is truthful and sufficient evidence proving that a decision for compulsory medical treatment should have been made, but the application was rejected, or that a decision for compulsory medical treatment should not have been made, but such a decision was made;
(6) There are serious violations of statutory adjudication procedure during the adjudication process that may affect the fair adjudication and decision.

Article 551 A People's Procuratorate shall exercise legal supervision over criminal proceedings, and where it discovers an illegal act, it shall, according to law, lodge a protest, issue a correction opinion, or make a procuratorial suggestion.
Where a People's Procuratorate suspects illegal facts, it may conduct investigation and verification by the following means:
(1) interrogating or questioning a criminal suspect;
(2) questioning a witness, victim, or other participant in the proceedings;
(3) questioning the case-handling personnel;
(4) questioning persons present or other persons who may have knowledge of the facts;
(5) hearing the opinions of a petitioner or complainant;
(6) hearing the opinions of a defender or duty lawyer;
(7) retrieving, inquiring into, or copying relevant registers, legal documents, medical examination records, case files and materials, etc.;
(8) retrieving interrogation transcripts, questioning transcripts, and related audio or video recordings or other audio-visual materials;
(9) conducting examination or evaluation of injuries or illnesses; and
(10) other means of investigation and verification.
During the process of investigation and verification, a People's Procuratorate shall not restrict the personal or property rights of the investigated subject.

Article 567 A People's Procuratorate shall supervise whether any of the following illegal acts exist in investigative activities:
(1) collecting confessions of a criminal suspect by torture or other illegal methods;
(2) failing to record or video-record interrogations of a criminal suspect when such recording or video-recording is required by law, or interrogating a criminal suspect outside of a lawful detention facility;
(3) collecting witness testimony or victim statements by violence, threats, illegal restriction of personal liberty, or other illegal methods, or preventing witnesses from testifying by violence, threats, or other methods, or inducing others to give false testimony;
(4) forging, concealing, destroying, substituting, or privately altering evidence, or helping a party to destroy or forge evidence;
(5) violating the provisions of the Criminal Procedure Law on deciding, implementing, changing, or revoking compulsory measures, or upon expiration of the lawful period of compulsory measures, failing to release, discharge, or change the measures;
(6) failing to return a security deposit for release on bail pending investigation or trial when it should be returned;
(7) violating the provisions of the Criminal Procedure Law on interrogation, questioning, inspection, examination, search, expert evaluation, use of technical investigation measures, etc.;
(8) sealing, seizing, or freezing property unrelated to the case, or failing to unseal, release, or unfreeze such property when it should be unsealed, released, or unfrozen;
(9) embezzling, misappropriating, privately distributing, substituting, or using sealed, seized, or frozen property and its fruits in violation of regulations;
(10) quashing a case when it should not be quashed;
(11) failure of an investigator to withdraw when withdrawal is required;
(12) failing to inform a criminal suspect of his or her procedural rights when such information should be given according to law, thereby affecting the criminal suspect's exercise of procedural rights;
(13) failing to notify the family of a criminal suspect when such notification is required by law after detention, arrest, or residential surveillance at a designated place;
(14) obstructing a party, defender, litigation agent, or duty lawyer from lawfully exercising procedural rights;
(15) failing to issue an explanation or provide evidentiary materials on the lawfulness of the collection of evidence when required; and
(16) other acts in investigative activities that violate legal provisions.

Article 570 A People's Procuratorate shall supervise whether any of the following illegal acts exist in adjudicative activities:
(1) a People's Court's acceptance of a criminal case violates the provisions on jurisdiction;
(2) a People's Court, when trying a case, violates the statutory time limits for trial and service;
(3) the composition of the tribunal does not comply with legal provisions, or a person who should withdraw according to provisions fails to do so;
(4) the court trial violates statutory procedures;
(5) infringing upon the procedural rights and other lawful rights of a party or other participants in proceedings;
(6) a decision made by the court during trial on a procedural matter violates legal provisions;
(7) ordering a retrial by way of a ruling in violation of legal provisions;
(8) intentionally destroying, altering, concealing, forging, or substituting evidence or other litigation materials, or deciding a case based on evidence that has not been investigated or cross-examined through statutory procedures;
(9) failing to investigate and collect relevant evidence when such collection should be done according to law;
(10) engaging in favoritism and bending the law, intentionally deciding a case contrary to facts and law;
(11) accepting or soliciting property or other benefits from a party, his or her near relatives, or a lawyer entrusted by the party, etc.;
(12) taking compulsory measures in violation of legal provisions, or upon expiration of the statutory period for compulsory measures, failing to release, discharge, or change the measures;
(13) failing to return a security deposit for release on bail pending investigation or trial when it should be returned;
(14) sealing, seizing, or freezing property unrelated to the case, or failing to unseal, release, or unfreeze such property when it should be unsealed, released, or unfrozen;
(15) embezzling, misappropriating, privately distributing, substituting, or using sealed, seized, or frozen property and its fruits in violation of regulations; and
(16) other acts that violate legal provisions.

Article 577 A People's Procuratorate may conduct a review of the necessity of custody by the following means:
(1) reviewing the reasons and supporting materials that the criminal suspect or defendant does not need to be kept in custody;
(2) hearing opinions of the criminal suspect or defendant and his or her legal representative and defender;
(3) hearing opinions of the victim and his or her legal representative and litigation agent, and ascertaining whether a settlement agreement has been reached;
(4) hearing opinions of the case-handling authority;
(5) investigating and verifying the physical condition of the criminal suspect or defendant;
(6) other methods that need to be taken.
When necessary, an open review may be conducted in accordance with relevant provisions.

Article 579 If a People's Procuratorate discovers that a criminal suspect or defendant falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall suggest to the case-handling authority that the person be released or that compulsory measures be changed:
(1) significant changes have occurred in the evidence of the case, and there is no evidence proving that a crime occurred or that the criminal act was committed by the criminal suspect or defendant;
(2) the facts or circumstances of the case have changed, and the criminal suspect or defendant may be sentenced to criminal detention, public surveillance, independent application of supplementary punishment, exemption from criminal punishment, or be found not guilty;
(3) if the criminal suspect or defendant is kept in custody, the custody period will exceed the term of imprisonment that may be legally imposed;
(4) the basic facts of the case have been ascertained, evidence has been collected and fixed, and conditions for release on bail pending investigation or trial or residential surveillance are met.

Article 580 Where a People's Procuratorate discovers that a criminal suspect or defendant falls under any of the following circumstances and shows remorse, and if not keeping the person in custody would not cause danger to society, it may suggest to the case-handling authority that the person be released or that compulsory measures be changed:
(1) a preparator or an offender who discontinues a crime;
(2) an accomplice or a coerced accomplice in a joint crime;
(3) an offender of a negligent crime;
(4) a person who commits excessive defense or acts beyond the limits of necessity;
(5) a first-time offender with relatively minor subjective malignancy;
(6) a minor or a person who has reached the age of seventy-five;
(7) who has, according to law, voluntarily reached a settlement agreement with the victim and has performed or provided security;
(8) who admits guilt and accepts punishment;
(9) who suffers from a serious illness and cannot take care of himself or herself;
(10) a pregnant woman or a woman nursing her own infant;
(11) who is the sole supporter of a person who cannot take care of himself or herself;
(12) who may be sentenced to fixed-term imprisonment of less than one year or be placed on probation;
(13) other circumstances where continued custody is unnecessary.

Article 584 If a People's Procuratorate finds that a first-instance judgment or ruling of a People's Court at the same level falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall lodge a protest:
(1) the facts determined are definitely erroneous or the evidence on which the conviction and sentencing are based is not reliable or sufficient;
(2) there is reliable and sufficient evidence proving that a guilty person was found not guilty, or an innocent person was found guilty;
(3) a serious crime is lightly punished, a light crime is heavily punished, or the application of penalty is manifestly improper;
(4) the charge is incorrect, and a single crime is punished as multiple crimes or multiple crimes are punished as a single crime, affecting the sentencing or causing serious social impact;
(5) errors in exempting from criminal punishment or applying probation, restraining orders, restrictions on commutation, etc.;
(6) the People's Court seriously violates the procedural rules provided by law in the course of trial.

Article 591 Where a People's Procuratorate finds that a legally effective judgment or ruling of a People's Court is indeed erroneous and falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall lodge a protest with the People's Court in accordance with the trial supervision procedure:
(1) new evidence proving that the facts determined in the original judgment or ruling are indeed erroneous and may affect the conviction and sentencing;
(2) the evidence based on which the conviction and sentencing were made is not reliable or sufficient;
(3) the evidence based on which the conviction and sentencing were made shall be excluded according to law;
(4) there are contradictions among the main evidence based on which the conviction and sentencing were made;
(5) the main factual basis of the original judgment or ruling has been lawfully modified or revoked;
(6) the determination of the crime is erroneous and obviously affects the sentencing;
(7) the provisions of the law on the statute of limitations for prosecution are violated;
(8) the sentencing is obviously improper;
(9) the procedural rules prescribed by law are violated, which may affect a fair trial; or
(10) the judicial personnel committed acts of embezzlement, bribery, favoritism, or perversion of justice in adjudicating the case.
Where a People's Procuratorate finds that a legally effective judgment or ruling of a People's Court at the same level may be erroneous, it shall separately assign a prosecutor or a case-handling group of prosecutors to conduct review. Upon review, if it finds that the case falls under any of the circumstances set forth in the preceding paragraph, it shall request the People's Procuratorate at the next higher level to lodge a protest.
Review of legally effective judgments or rulings shall be conducted by reference to the provisions of Article 585 of these Rules.

Article 603 The Supreme People's Procuratorate and the People's Procuratorates at the provincial level shall exercise legal supervision over death penalty review activities by handling the following cases:
(1) death penalty review cases of which the People's Court notifies the People's Procuratorate;
(2) death penalty review cases for which a People's Procuratorate at a lower level requests supervision or reports major circumstances;
(3) death penalty review cases for which a party, his or her near relative, or a retained lawyer applies for supervision to the People's Procuratorate; and
(4) other death penalty review cases that are deemed to require supervision.

Article 604 Where a People's Procuratorate at the provincial level, in a case that has entered the death penalty review procedure of the Supreme People's Court, finds that the case falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall promptly request supervision from the Supreme People's Procuratorate:
(1) the facts of the case are unclear or the evidence is insufficient, and the case shall be remanded for retrial or the judgment shall be modified according to law;
(2) the defendant has circumstances for lenient punishment and shall not be sentenced to death according to law;
(3) the application of law is erroneous;
(4) the procedural rules prescribed by law are violated, which may affect a fair trial; or
(5) other circumstances that require a request for supervision.

Article 609 The review of death penalty review supervision cases may be conducted by the following means:
(1) reviewing the materials transferred by the People's Court, the relevant case files submitted by the People's Procuratorate at the lower level, and the materials submitted by the party, his or her near relatives, or the retained lawyer;
(2) requesting the case review report, the bill of prosecution opinion, the opinion of appearance in court, and other documents from the People's Procuratorate at the lower level to understand the relevant circumstances of the case;
(3) requesting or consulting the case files from the People's Court;
(4) verifying or entrusting others to verify the main evidence;
(5) interrogating the defendant and hearing the opinions of the retained lawyer;
(6) consulting specialized institutions or persons with specialized knowledge on technical issues, or entrusting them to review evidence; and
(7) other means as required.

Article 610 Where, in reviewing a death penalty review supervision case, any of the following circumstances exists, the opinions of the People's Procuratorate at the lower level shall be heard:
(1) there are doubts about the main facts or evidence of the case;
(2) there is significant dispute over the application of the death penalty;
(3) the case may give rise to major risks in judicial work; or
(4) other circumstances where opinions shall be heard.

Article 611 Where the Supreme People's Procuratorate, upon review, finds that a death penalty review case falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall, with the approval of the Procurator-General, submit a procuratorial opinion to the Supreme People's Court in accordance with law:
(1) It considers that the imposition of the death penalty is improper, or the facts of the case are unclear, the evidence is insufficient, and the death penalty shall not be approved according to law;
(2) It considers that the grounds for disapproval of the death penalty are not established, and the death penalty shall be approved according to law;
(3) New facts and evidence are discovered that may affect the conviction and sentencing of the defendant;
(4) There is a serious violation of the litigation procedures prescribed by law, which may affect a fair trial;
(5) Judicial personnel, in handling the case, engage in acts such as embezzlement, bribery, favoritism, malpractice, and perversion of justice;
(6) Other circumstances requiring the submission of a procuratorial opinion.
Where the Supreme People's Procuratorate agrees with the Supreme People's Court's opinion on approval or disapproval, it shall, with the approval of the Procurator-General, respond in writing to the Supreme People's Court.
For a case where a provincial-level People's Procuratorate requests supervision or reports a major situation, if the Supreme People's Procuratorate considers that there are circumstances affecting the application of the death penalty, it shall promptly transfer the relevant materials to the Supreme People's Court.

Article 614 In the process of handling a case by a People's Procuratorate, where a criminal suspect or defendant is in custody and falls under any of the following circumstances, the case-handling department shall, within ten days after making a decision or receiving a written decision or ruling, notify the department of the same procuratorate responsible for supervision duties:
(1) Approving or deciding to extend the period of investigation custody;
(2) For a case directly accepted for investigation by the People's Procuratorate, deciding to recalculate the period of investigation custody, alter, or rescind compulsory measures;
(3) Conducting a psychiatric evaluation on the criminal suspect or defendant;
(4) During the period of examination for prosecution, changing jurisdiction or extending the period of examination for prosecution;
(5) The case is returned for supplementary investigation, or upon completion of supplementary investigation and transfer for prosecution, recalculating the period of examination for prosecution;
(6) The People's Court decides to apply summary procedures or expedited procedures for trial of a first-instance case, or converts the case from summary procedures to ordinary procedures, or from expedited procedures to summary procedures or ordinary procedures for retrial;
(7) The People's Court changes jurisdiction, decides to postpone trial or suspend trial, or agrees to the People's Procuratorate's withdrawal of prosecution.

Article 615 Where a People's Procuratorate finds that a detention center's management activities regarding custody periods fall under any of the following circumstances, it shall submit a corrective opinion in accordance with law:
(1) Failing to promptly urge the case-handling organ to handle the procedures for transfer of custody;
(2) Failing to issue a notice to the case-handling organ that the custody period is about to expire within seven days before the expiration of the custody period of the criminal suspect or defendant;
(3) After the criminal suspect or defendant is held in custody beyond the period, failing to immediately report in writing to the People's Procuratorate and notify the case-handling organ;
(4) After receiving an application, petition, or complaint submitted by the criminal suspect or defendant, or their legal representative, close relative, or defender, for alteration of compulsory measures, review of necessity of custody, or request for release or alteration of compulsory measures upon expiration of the custody period, failing to promptly forward it to the relevant case-handling organ or People's Procuratorate;
(5) Other illegal circumstances.

Article 616 Where a People's Procuratorate finds that a public security organ's implementation of the period of investigation custody falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall submit a corrective opinion in accordance with law:
(1) Failing to handle the procedures for transfer of custody in accordance with regulations;
(2) Deciding to recalculate the period of investigation custody or having the period of investigation custody extended upon approval, without notifying the People's Procuratorate and the detention center in writing;
(3) Conducting a psychiatric evaluation on a criminal suspect without notifying the People's Procuratorate and the detention center in writing;
(4) Other illegal circumstances.

Article 617 Where a People's Procuratorate finds that a People's Court's implementation of the trial period falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall submit a corrective opinion in accordance with law:
(1) Failing to handle the procedures for transfer of custody in accordance with regulations during the first instance, second instance, and death penalty review stages;
(2) Recalculating the trial period, approving an extension of the trial period, changing jurisdiction, postponing trial, suspending trial, or remanding for retrial in violation of the Criminal Procedure Law;
(3) Deciding to recalculate the trial period, approving an extension of the trial period, changing jurisdiction, postponing trial, suspending trial, or conducting a psychiatric evaluation on the defendant, without notifying the People's Procuratorate and the detention center in writing;
(4) Other illegal circumstances.

Article 624 When exercising supervision over the execution of punishment and regulatory and law enforcement activities, People's Procuratorates may handle the matter respectively according to the following circumstances:
(1) Where a minor defect in law enforcement, a safety hazard, or a minor illegal act is discovered, an oral corrective opinion shall be given and recorded in the case file;
(2) Where a serious illegal act is discovered, a major accident occurs, or the matter is not corrected within seven days after an oral corrective opinion is given, a written corrective opinion shall be submitted;
(3) Where issues that may lead to unfair law enforcement, or major regulatory loopholes, major safety hazards, major accident risks, etc., are discovered, a procuratorial recommendation shall be made.
The implementation of rectification of the issues and leads discovered in the preceding paragraph during patrol inspections shall be supervised through patrol inspections.

Article 625 Where a People's Procuratorate finds that the delivery for execution activities of a People's Court, public security organ, detention center, or other organ falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall submit a corrective opinion in accordance with law:
(1) The first-instance People's Court that delivers the criminal for execution fails to serve legal documents such as the judgment, ruling, copy of the indictment of the People's Procuratorate, copy of the private prosecution complaint, execution notice, and case closing registration form to enforcement organs such as the public security organ, prison, and community correction institution within the statutory time limit;
(2) For a criminal sentenced to death sentence with a two-year reprieve, life imprisonment, or fixed-term imprisonment with a remaining term of more than three months, the public security organ or detention center fails, within thirty days after receiving legal documents such as the execution notice from the People's Court, to deliver an adult criminal to prison for execution of the punishment, or fails to deliver a juvenile criminal to a juvenile correctional facility for execution of the punishment;
(3) For a criminal who needs to be taken into custody for execution of the punishment but was not in custody before the judgment or ruling takes effect, the first-instance People's Court fails to promptly take the criminal into custody and deliver him to the public security organ, and fails to serve legal documents such as the judgment, ruling, and execution notice to the public security organ;
(4) After receiving legal documents such as the judgment, ruling, and execution notice from the People's Court for a criminal who needs to be taken into custody for execution of the punishment but whose whereabouts are unknown, the public security organ fails to promptly arrest or issue a wanted order;
(5) For a criminal sentenced to public surveillance, given a suspended sentence, or for whom the People's Court decides to temporarily serve the sentence outside prison, after the judgment or ruling takes effect or after receiving the People's Court's decision on temporary service of sentence outside prison, the criminal is not delivered to the community correction institution at the criminal's place of residence for execution in accordance with law; or for a criminal solely sentenced to deprivation of political rights, after the judgment or ruling takes effect, the criminal is not delivered to the public security organ at the criminal's place of residence for execution in accordance with law; or the People's Court delivers the criminal for execution in accordance with law, but the community correction institution or public security organ refuses to accept the criminal when it should;
(6) Other illegal circumstances.

Article 629 Where a People's Procuratorate finds that the activities of a People's Court, prison, detention center, or public security organ regarding temporary service of sentence outside prison fall under any of the following circumstances, it shall submit a corrective opinion in accordance with law:
(1) Proposing or deciding on temporary service of sentence outside prison for a criminal who does not meet the statutory conditions;
(2) The procedures for proposing or deciding on temporary service of sentence outside prison violate legal provisions or lack complete lawful formalities, or for a criminal who needs medical parole, there is no diagnosis certificate or supporting documents issued by a hospital designated by the provincial-level people's government;
(3) A prison or detention center submits a written opinion on temporary service of sentence outside prison without simultaneously sending a copy of the written opinion to the People's Procuratorate;
(4) After a criminal is decided or approved for temporary service of sentence outside prison, the criminal is not delivered to the community correction institution at the criminal's place of residence for community correction in accordance with law;
(5) Failing to propose temporary service of sentence outside prison in accordance with law for a criminal who meets the conditions for temporary service of sentence outside prison;
(6) The People's Court fails to seek the opinion of the People's Procuratorate in accordance with law before making a decision on temporary service of sentence outside prison;
(7) Discovering that a criminal does not meet the conditions for temporary service of sentence outside prison, seriously violates the regulatory provisions on temporary service of sentence outside prison during the period of such service, or the conditions for temporary service of sentence outside prison cease to exist and the term of imprisonment has not expired, and the criminal should be taken back into custody but is not promptly taken back into custody;
(8) After the People's Court decides to take a criminal on temporary service of sentence outside prison back into custody and delivers the relevant legal documents to the public security organ, prison, or detention center, the prison or detention center fails to promptly take the criminal back into custody;
(9) For a criminal who does not meet the conditions for temporary service of sentence outside prison but is allowed such service through illegal means such as bribery or fraud, or for a criminal who escapes during the period of temporary service of sentence outside prison, the prison or detention center fails to propose to the People's Court that the period of service outside prison or the period of escape shall not be counted toward the term of imprisonment, or the proposal regarding the calculation of the criminal's term of imprisonment is illegal or improper;
(10) Upon expiration of the term of imprisonment of a criminal on temporary service of sentence outside prison, failing to promptly handle the release procedures;
(11) Other illegal circumstances.

Article 631 Upon receiving the decision or the temporary execution outside prison decision document copied and transmitted by the deciding or approving authority, the People's Procuratorate shall promptly examine the following:
(1) whether the convict is one sentenced to fixed-term imprisonment or criminal detention;
(2) whether the convict is one with a serious disease requiring medical treatment outside prison;
(3) whether the convict is a pregnant woman or a woman nursing her own infant;
(4) whether the convict is unable to take care of himself/herself and temporary execution outside prison would not endanger society;
(5) whether the convict is one for whom medical treatment outside prison may pose a social danger, or one who has self-inflicted injuries or disabilities;
(6) whether the deciding or approving authority complies with the provisions of Article 265, paragraph 5 of the Criminal Procedure Law;
(7) whether the handling of temporary execution outside prison complies with the statutory procedures.

Article 636 If the People's Procuratorate discovers that the activities of a prison or other executing authority in submitting a recommendation to the People's Court for a ruling on commutation of punishment or parole fall under any of the following circumstances, it shall present corrective opinions according to law:
(1) submitting a recommendation to the People's Court for a ruling on commutation of punishment or parole for a convict who does not meet the statutory conditions for commutation of punishment or parole;
(2) failing to submit a recommendation to the People's Court for a ruling on commutation of punishment or parole for a convict who should have commutation of punishment or parole according to law;
(3) submitting a recommendation for commutation of punishment or parole for a convict in violation of statutory procedures, or without complete legal formalities;
(4) the extent of commutation, starting time, interval, or the interval between commutation and subsequent parole recommended for a convict do not conform to relevant provisions;
(5) the actual sentence to be served by a convict after commutation, or the parole probation period, as recommended, do not conform to relevant legal provisions;
(6) other illegal circumstances.

Article 638 After receiving a copy of the ruling on commutation of punishment or parole from the People's Court, the People's Procuratorate shall promptly examine the following:
(1) whether the convict who has been commuted or paroled meets the statutory conditions, and whether the extent of commutation, starting time, interval, or the interval between commutation and subsequent parole, the actual sentence to be served after commutation, or the parole probation period conform to relevant provisions;
(2) whether the procedures for the executing authority's submission of the recommendation for commutation of punishment or parole are lawful;
(3) whether the procedures for the People's Court's hearing and ruling on commutation of punishment or parole are lawful;
(4) whether the People's Court's ruling not to grant commutation of punishment or parole to the convict conforms to relevant provisions;
(5) whether the People's Court's ruling on commutation of punishment or parole has been served to the executing authority according to law and published to the public.

Article 643 If the People's Procuratorate discovers that community correction enforcement activities have any of the following circumstances, it shall present corrective opinions according to law:
(1) after the community correction subject reports, the community correction institution fails to perform the statutory notification obligation, causing the subject not to accept supervision and management in accordance with relevant provisions;
(2) approving, in violation of legal provisions, the community correction subject to leave the city or county where he/she resides, or approving the subject to enter a specific area or place in violation of the content of the People's Court's restraining order;
(3) failing to supervise and manage according to law, resulting in the community correction subject being out of supervision;
(4) when the community correction subject violates supervision and management regulations or the People's Court's restraining order, failing to give a warning according to law, or failing to submit a recommendation to the public security organ for public security penalties;
(5) committing acts that infringe upon the lawful rights of the community correction subject, such as beating, corporal punishment, maltreatment, humiliation of personality, or forcing him/her to participate in community service exceeding the time limit or physical capacity;
(6) failing to handle the release or termination of community correction according to law;
(7) other illegal circumstances.

Article 644 If the People's Procuratorate discovers that the activities of changing the execution of criminal punishment for a community correction subject have any of the following circumstances, it shall present corrective opinions according to law:
(1) the community correction institution fails to submit a recommendation to the People's Court, public security organ, or prison administration authority for revocation of probation, revocation of parole, or for committing a convict under temporary execution outside prison to prison, or fails to submit a recommendation for commutation of punishment to the People's Court according to law;
(2) the People's Court, public security organ, or prison administration authority fails to make a ruling or decision according to law, or fails to serve it according to law;
(3) the public security organ fails to deliver the convict to a detention center or prison according to law, or the detention center or prison fails to commit the convict to prison according to law;
(4) the public security organ fails to pursue and arrest a fugitive convict according to law;
(5) other illegal circumstances.

Article 645 If the People's Procuratorate discovers that the People's Court's enforcement of the property-related part of criminal judgments has any of the following circumstances, it shall present corrective opinions according to law:
(1) the case-filing activity for enforcement is illegal;
(2) the extension of time for payment, discretionary reduction or exemption of a fine is illegal;
(3) the suspension of enforcement or termination of enforcement is illegal;
(4) the person subject to enforcement has the capacity to perform but should perform without performing;
(5) infringing upon the lawful rights and interests of the person subject to enforcement, victim, interested party, or person not involved in the case;
(6) after the criminal judgment is wholly or partially revoked, failing to return or compensate according to law;
(7) the property enforced is not turned over to the state treasury according to law;
(8) other illegal circumstances.
In supervising the People's Court's enforcement of the property-related part of criminal judgments, the People's Procuratorate may investigate and verify with the relevant entities or individuals regarding the public security organ's sealing, seizure, and freezing of case-related property; the transfer, case-filing, and enforcement activities of the People's Court's trial department, case-filing department, and enforcement department; and the performance capacity of the person subject to enforcement.

Article 649 Before the execution of a death sentence, if the People's Procuratorate discovers any of the following circumstances, it shall recommend that the People's Court immediately suspend the execution and report level by level to the Supreme People's Procuratorate's department responsible for supervision of death sentence review:
(1) the person subject to execution is not the convict who should be executed;
(2) the convict was under the age of 18 at the time of the crime, or was 75 years of age or older at the time of trial, and should not be sentenced to death according to law;
(3) the convict is pregnant;
(4) another criminal suspect in the joint crime has been brought to justice, or another convict in the joint crime has been suspended or stopped from execution, which may affect the sentencing of the convict;
(5) the convict may have committed other crimes;
(6) the convict has disclosed a major criminal fact of another person or has other major meritorious service, which may require a changed sentence;
(7) the judgment or ruling may have other errors affecting the determination of guilt or sentencing.
During the execution of the death sentence, if the People's Procuratorate discovers that the People's Court has violated the personal or property rights of the convict being executed, or the lawful rights of his/her close relatives or heirs, it shall present corrective opinions according to law.

Article 654 Where the People's Procuratorate discovers any of the following circumstances in the admission activities of a detention center or a prison, it shall put forward corrective opinions according to law:
(1) There is no admission document or certificate, the documents or certificates are incomplete, or the admitted person does not match the documents or certificates;
(2) A person who should be admitted according to law is not admitted, or a person who should not be detained according to law is admitted;
(3) The admitted person is not informed of his or her rights and obligations;
(4) Other illegal circumstances.

Article 658 Where the People's Procuratorate discovers that a detention center violates the relevant provisions and has any of the following circumstances, it shall put forward corrective opinions according to law:
(1) Disclosing information to detained persons, privately transmitting letters or articles, assisting in forging, destroying, or concealing evidence, or interfering with witness testimony or collusion of confessions;
(2) Violating the provisions by agreeing to allow investigators to bring a criminal suspect out of the detention center for interrogation;
(3) Upon receiving an application for modification of compulsory measures or other applications, petitions, accusations, or reports from a detained criminal suspect or defendant, his or her legal representative, near relative, or defender, failing to timely transfer or notify the People's Procuratorate or the relevant case-handling organ;
(4) Failing to arrange for a defense lawyer to meet with a detained criminal suspect or defendant according to law when such arrangement should be made;
(5) Illegally arranging for a defense lawyer or other person to meet with a detained criminal suspect or defendant;
(6) Monitoring a defense lawyer's meeting with a criminal suspect or defendant;
(7) Other illegal circumstances.

Article 659 Where the People's Procuratorate discovers any of the following circumstances in the activities of a detention center in executing punishments on behalf of a prison, it shall put forward corrective opinions according to law:
(1) Retaining a criminal sentenced to fixed-term imprisonment with a remaining term of more than three months in the detention center to serve the sentence;
(2) Mixing the custody, management, or education of criminals serving sentences in the detention center with criminal suspects or defendants;
(3) Other illegal circumstances.

Article 661 Where the People's Procuratorate discovers any of the following circumstances in the release activities of a detention center or a prison, it shall put forward corrective opinions according to law:
(1) There is no release document or certificate, the documents or certificates are incomplete, or the released person does not match the documents or certificates;
(2) A person who should be released is not released, a person who should not be released is released, or the release notice is not served according to the provisions;
(3) For detained persons who are taken out, escorted, or transferred out of the detention center, or for criminals who are granted special leave from prison, temporary leave from prison, transfer to another prison, or temporary execution of sentence outside prison, no personnel are assigned to escort them and handle the handover procedures according to the provisions;
(4) Other illegal circumstances.

Article 662 Where the People's Procuratorate discovers any of the following circumstances in a facility such as a detention center, a prison, or a compulsory medical institution, it shall conduct an accident inspection:
(1) A person under supervision or a person subjected to compulsory medical treatment dies abnormally, is injured or disabled, or escapes;
(2) A person under supervision disrupts the supervision order, where the circumstances are serious;
(3) A public health emergency occurs;
(4) Other major accidents.
If a person under supervision or a person subjected to compulsory medical treatment dies abnormally, a circuit inspection shall be organized.

Article 663 The People's Procuratorate shall review the accident investigation conclusions of a facility such as a detention center, a prison, or a compulsory medical institution, or the competent authority. Under any of the following circumstances, the People's Procuratorate shall investigate and verify:
(1) The person under supervision or the person subjected to compulsory medical treatment, his or her legal representative, or near relative has an objection to the investigation conclusion, and the People's Procuratorate deems it necessary to investigate;
(2) The People's Procuratorate has an objection to the investigation conclusion;
(3) Other circumstances requiring investigation.
The People's Procuratorate shall notify the custodial facility or the competent authority and the near relative of the person under supervision or the person subjected to compulsory medical treatment in writing of the conclusion of the investigation and verification. If it considers that the handling opinion of the custodial facility or the competent authority is improper, or there is a problem in supervision and law enforcement, it shall put forward a corrective opinion or a procuratorial suggestion; if it considers that there may be illegal or criminal circumstances, it shall transfer the matter to the relevant department for handling.

Article 665 If the case management department of a People's Procuratorate discovers that any of the following circumstances exists in the case-handling activities of this procuratorate, it shall promptly issue a corrective opinion:
(1) The sealing, seizure, freezing, custody, or disposal of assets involved in the case does not conform to relevant laws and regulations;
(2) The production or use of legal documents does not conform to laws and relevant regulations;
(3) Violation of provisions on custody periods or case-handling periods;
(4) Infringement upon the procedural rights of parties, defenders, or litigation agents;
(5) Failure to perform the duty of legal supervision over illegal acts in litigation activities such as case filing, investigation, review of arrest, public prosecution, adjudication, and enforcement activities according to law;
(6) Other circumstances where a corrective opinion shall be issued.
If the circumstances are minor, an oral reminder may be given; if the circumstances are relatively serious, a case process monitoring notice shall be sent, reminding the handling department to promptly ascertain the situation and make corrections; if the circumstances are serious, the matter shall also be reported to the Chief Procurator at the same time.
After receiving the case process monitoring notice, the handling department shall reply in writing to the case management department regarding the verification within ten days.

Article 677 After receiving the written request for criminal judicial assistance and the attached materials forwarded by the external liaison authority, the Supreme People's Procuratorate shall, upon review, handle the matter as follows:
(1) Where it considers, in accordance with the provisions of the International Criminal Judicial Assistance Law and the criminal judicial assistance treaty, that assistance may be executed, it shall make a decision and arrange for the relevant provincial people's procuratorate to execute it;
(2) Where it considers, in accordance with the provisions of the International Criminal Judicial Assistance Law or the criminal judicial assistance treaty, that assistance shall be wholly or partially refused, it shall return the request and the attached materials to the external liaison authority and state the reasons;
(3) Where there are confidentiality requirements or other additional conditions for the execution of the request, it shall raise them through the external liaison authority to the foreign country, and after the foreign country accepts the conditions and makes a written assurance, decide to execute conditionally;
(4) Where supplementary materials are needed, it shall, in writing, request the requesting party through the external liaison authority to provide them within a reasonable time limit.
